 "posts": [
  {
   "slug": "how-to-brush-properly",
   "title": "Are you brushing right? A two-minute guide",
   "date": "2026-10-23T09:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "everyday-care"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "brushing",
    "habits"
   ],
   "featured_media": "toothbrush",
   "excerpt": "Most of us brush every day, but few of us brush well. Here is the technique our hygienists teach.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You have probably brushed your teeth more than ten thousand times. Even so, our hygienists see the same missed spots every day: the gum line, the backs of the front teeth and the last molar on each side.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The two-minute routine</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} --><ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Hold the brush at a 45° angle to the gum line.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Use small, gentle circles, not hard scrubbing.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Spend 30 seconds on each quarter of your mouth.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Finish with the tongue to freshen your breath.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"tip-bubble\",\"layout\":{\"type\":\"constrained\"}} --><div class=\"wp-block-group tip-bubble\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Tip:</strong> brush before breakfast, or wait 30 minutes after eating. Acidic food softens enamel for a while.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Manual or electric?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Both work when used properly. An electric brush with a two-minute timer helps many people brush longer and more gently, which is why we often recommend one.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Pressing harder does not clean better. A soft brush and patience beat a hard brush every time.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Sofia, dental hygienist</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->",
   "sticky": true,
   "format": "standard",
   "comments": [
    {
     "author": "Hannah W.",
     "email": "hannah@example.com",
     "content": "I didn't know about waiting after breakfast! Changing my routine today.",
     "date": "2026-10-24T08:30:00"
    },
    {
     "author": "Leo P.",
     "email": "leo@example.com",
     "content": "My kids love the 30-seconds-per-corner game.",
     "date": "2026-10-25T19:10:00"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "slug": "first-dental-visit-for-kids",
   "title": "Your child's first dental visit: what to expect",
   "date": "2026-10-12T10:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "kids"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "children",
    "first-visit"
   ],
   "featured_media": "kids",
   "excerpt": "Book it by the first birthday. Here is how to make it fun, short and tear-free.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We recommend a first visit by your child's first birthday, or when the first tooth appears. It is short, playful and mostly about getting to know us.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Before the visit</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"fun-list\"} --><ul class=\"wp-block-list fun-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Read a picture book about the dentist together.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Play dentist at home with a toothbrush and a mirror.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Avoid words like 'hurt' or 'needle', even to reassure.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">During the visit</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Your child sits on your lap or rides up and down in the chair. We count teeth, look at the gums and give you tips for brushing at home. Most first visits take about fifteen minutes.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The goal of the first visit is not perfect teeth. It is a child who wants to come back.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Dr. Aria Holloway</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->",
   "sticky": false,
   "format": "standard",
   "comments": [
    {
     "author": "Mona T.",
     "email": "mona@example.com",
     "content": "Our daughter's first visit was exactly like this. She talked about the chair ride for a week!",
     "date": "2026-10-13T16:20:00"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "slug": "flossing-myths",
   "title": "Five flossing myths, gently busted",
   "date": "2026-09-29T09:30:00",
   "categories": [
    "everyday-care"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "flossing",
    "habits"
   ],
   "featured_media": "floss",
   "excerpt": "Bleeding gums? Not enough time? We answer the excuses we hear most often.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Flossing is the habit everyone means to start. Here are the five reasons we hear most for skipping it, and why they don't quite hold up.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} --><ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>'My gums bleed, so I stop.'</strong> Bleeding usually means the gums need flossing. It stops within two weeks.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>'I don't have time.'</strong> It takes about ninety seconds.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>'My teeth are too tight.'</strong> Try waxed floss or tape.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>'Water flossers don't count.'</strong> They do, and they are great with braces.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>'Brushing is enough.'</strong> A brush can't reach between teeth.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Make it stick</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Keep floss next to your toothbrush, floss before you brush and pair it with something you already do every evening. In a month it will feel strange to skip.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Not sure you are doing it right? Ask Sofia for a two-minute demo at your next cleaning.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "sticky": false,
   "format": "standard",
   "comments": []
  },
  {
   "slug": "clear-aligners-questions",
   "title": "Clear aligners: your questions answered",
   "date": "2026-09-15T11:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "orthodontics"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "aligners"
   ],
   "featured_media": "aligner",
   "excerpt": "How long, how much and does it hurt? Dr. Marlowe answers the questions he hears every week.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Clear aligners straighten teeth with a series of thin, removable trays. They are nearly invisible and you take them out to eat. Here are the questions we hear most.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How long does it take?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Most adult cases take six to eighteen months. Mild crowding can be done in as little as four. We show you a 3D preview of the result before you start.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Does it hurt?</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Each new tray feels tight for a day or two. Most people describe it as pressure rather than pain.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:table --><figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Aligners</th><th>Fixed braces</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Visible</td><td>Barely</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Removable</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Wear time</td><td>22 hours a day</td><td>Always</td></tr><tr><td>Check-ups</td><td>Every 8 weeks</td><td>Every 4–6 weeks</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The best results come from patients who wear their trays every day. The plastic only works when it's in your mouth.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Dr. Kenji Marlowe</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->",
   "sticky": false,
   "format": "standard",
   "comments": []
  },
  {
   "slug": "tooth-friendly-snacks",
   "title": "Tooth-friendly snacks for school lunch boxes",
   "date": "2026-09-02T08:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "kids",
    "everyday-care"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "nutrition",
    "children"
   ],
   "featured_media": "snacks",
   "excerpt": "Crunchy, cheesy and water-first: snacks that kids like and teeth love.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is not just how much sugar children eat, but how often. Every sugary snack starts a twenty-minute acid attack on the enamel. Fewer, better snacks make a big difference.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Our favourites</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"fun-list\"} --><ul class=\"wp-block-list fun-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Apple slices and carrot sticks: crunchy foods help clean teeth.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Cheese cubes: they help neutralise acid.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Plain yogurt with a few berries.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Water instead of juice, every time.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"tip-bubble\",\"layout\":{\"type\":\"constrained\"}} --><div class=\"wp-block-group tip-bubble\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Sticky sweets</strong> like dried fruit cling to teeth for hours. If they are in the lunch box, pack them with lunch rather than as a separate snack.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Ask us at your child's next check-up for a free lunch-box sticker sheet.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "sticky": false,
   "format": "standard",
   "comments": [
    {
     "author": "Ravi N.",
     "email": "ravi@example.com",
     "content": "The cheese tip is a winner in our house.",
     "date": "2026-09-04T12:45:00"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "slug": "is-whitening-safe",
   "title": "Is teeth whitening safe? What you should know",
   "date": "2026-08-19T10:30:00",
   "categories": [
    "cosmetic"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "whitening"
   ],
   "featured_media": "after",
   "excerpt": "Professional whitening is safe and effective. Here is how it differs from strips and whitening toothpaste.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A brighter smile is one of the most common requests we hear. The good news: when supervised by a dentist, whitening is safe for most adults.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Your options</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:table --><figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>How white</th><th>How long</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Whitening toothpaste</td><td>Removes surface stains</td><td>Ongoing</td></tr><tr><td>Shop strips</td><td>1–2 shades</td><td>2–4 weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Take-home trays</td><td>3–6 shades</td><td>2 weeks</td></tr><tr><td>In-clinic session</td><td>4–8 shades</td><td>1 hour</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Before you whiten</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"fun-list\"} --><ul class=\"wp-block-list fun-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Have a check-up: cavities and gum problems should be treated first.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Crowns and fillings do not whiten, so plan any replacements afterwards.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Expect mild sensitivity for a day or two.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Avoid coffee, tea and red wine for 48 hours after each session to keep the result.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Results usually last one to three years. A short top-up with your take-home trays keeps the shade.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "sticky": false,
   "format": "standard",
   "comments": []
  },
  {
   "slug": "dental-emergency-what-to-do",
   "title": "Knocked-out tooth? What to do in the first hour",
   "date": "2026-08-05T09:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "everyday-care"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "emergencies"
   ],
   "featured_media": "clinic",
   "excerpt": "A knocked-out adult tooth can often be saved if you act fast. Keep this guide handy.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Accidents happen, especially in summer. If an adult tooth is knocked out, the first hour matters most.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Step by step</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} --><ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Pick the tooth up by the crown, never the root.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Rinse it briefly in milk or water. Do not scrub.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Try to put it back in the socket and bite on a clean cloth.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>If you can't, keep it in milk or inside the cheek.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Call us straight away. We keep emergency slots every morning.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"tip-bubble\",\"layout\":{\"type\":\"constrained\"}} --><div class=\"wp-block-group tip-bubble\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Baby teeth</strong> should not be put back. Bring your child in so we can check the gum and the adult tooth underneath.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Milk is the best emergency kit there is. Every school bag should know that.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Dr. Aria Holloway</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->",
   "sticky": false,
   "format": "standard",
   "comments": []
  },
  {
   "slug": "sensitive-teeth-causes",
   "title": "Why are my teeth sensitive to cold?",
   "date": "2026-07-22T10:00:00",
   "categories": [
    "everyday-care"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "habits"
   ],
   "featured_media": "before",
   "excerpt": "That sudden zing with ice cream has a cause, and usually a simple fix.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A short, sharp twinge with cold drinks or sweet food is one of the most common things people tell us about. It usually means the softer layer under the enamel, called dentine, has become exposed.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Common causes</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"fun-list\"} --><ul class=\"wp-block-list fun-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Brushing too hard, which wears enamel and pushes gums back.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Acidic drinks such as fizzy drinks, juice and sports drinks.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Grinding your teeth at night.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A small crack or an old filling.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What helps</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A soft brush, a sensitivity toothpaste used for a few weeks and fewer acidic drinks solve most cases. If the pain lingers or is in one tooth only, book a check-up so we can rule out a cavity.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you grind at night, a thin custom night guard protects your enamel while you sleep.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "sticky": false,
   "format": "standard",
   "comments": []
  }
 ],
